Recommend approval:
1. To accept the Florida Department of Transportation under the Section 5310 Capital Grant Agreement for Fiscal Year 2026;
2. To procure vehicles under the State's bid list through FDOT's Transit Research Inspection Procurement Services (TRIPS) Program;
3. To adopt an Unanticipated Revenue Resolution (URR) to accept and appropriate $828,138 for the Florida Department of Transportation's Section 5310 Capital Grant Agreement; and
4. Of a budget transfer in the amount of $92,016 from Transit Reserves for Operations to Machinery and Equipment.
The fiscal impact is $920,154 - $828,138 (revenue/expenditure - grant funded) and $92,016 (expenditure - county match).

Recommend approval:
1. To accept the Emergency Management Performance Grant (EMPG) Agreement;
2. To execute the EMPG Agreement for Fiscal Year 2025-2026 in the amount of $104,126.45;
3. To authorize the County Manager to execute future amendments/modifications that do not involve a financial impact;
4. To authorize the Director of the Office of Emergency Management to execute Exhibit 1 - Federal Programs and Resources Awarded (page 29);
5. To authorize the Director of the Office of Emergency Management to execute Exhibit 2 - Certification Regarding Telecommunications and Video Restrictions (pages 30 and 31); and
6. To authorize the Director of the Office of Emergency Management to execute Exhibit 3 - Certification Regarding Lobbying (page 32).
The fiscal impact is $104,126.45 (revenue/expenditure - grant funded).

Recommend approval:
1. Of Contract 26-903 with Qualis General Contracting, LLC (Tampa, FL) for the Supervisor of Elections Building Commercial Tenant Improvements;
2. Of a budget transfer in the amount of $871,991 from Buildings to Leasehold Improvements;
3. Of a budget transfer in the amount of $87,149 from Repair and Maintenance to Leasehold Improvements; and
4. To authorize the Office of Procurement Services to execute all supporting documentation.
The estimated fiscal impact is $3,119,387 (expenditure — $2,835,807 plus $283,580 for contingency purposes). Commission District 3.

Recommend retroactive approval:
1. Of Contract 25-743C proposal for the repair of the sinkhole and associated roadway damage on Empire Church Road;
Recommend approval:
2. To adopt an Unanticipated Revenue Resolution (URR) to accept and appropriate $533,577 to the Infrastructure Sales Tax Fund for repairs on Empire Church Road;
3. Of a budget transfer in the amount of $1,000,000 from Facilities Capital Buildings to Public Works Capital Infrastructure Construction;
4. Of a budget transfer in the amount of $1,000,000 from Gas Tax Reserves for Operations to Infrastructure Construction; and
5. To authorize the Office of Procurement Services to execute all supporting documentation related to the contract for road repairs; and
6. Approval for the Chairman to execute the required Right of Entries for the two adjacent property owners, which include indemnification language.
The estimated fiscal impact is $2,533,576.44. Commission District 1.

Recommend approval:
1. Of a Real Estate Purchase and Sales Agreement for Property with Donald E. Rhames and Laura Mae Rhames;
2. To authorize the County Manager to approve any amendments to the Purchase Agreement that do not have a fiscal impact, including but not limited to extended possession for Donald E. Rhames as allowed by Florida Department of Transportation (FDOT)'s Residential Relocation and Moving Assistance under the Florida Relocation Assistance (Relocation Assistance) Program;
3. To authorize the County Manager to approve any required expenditures of relocation benefits to the eligible displaced individual(s) pursuant to FDOT's Relocation Assistance Program guidelines and requirements;
4. To authorize the Public Works staff to work post-closing on the encumbrances with the County Attorney's Office; and
5. To authorization for Chairman to execute any necessary closing documents.
The estimated fiscal impact is $550,000 plus due diligence, closing costs, and relocation benefits (expenditure) and is within, and will not exceed, the Fiscal Year 2026 Budget. The total cost for relocation benefits, which may include rental assistance or purchase assistance and any moving expenses, is not determinable at this time. Commission District 2.

Recommend approval:
1. Of a Purchase Agreement for Property with WDL Property Holdings 8 LLC for the Britt Road Repair Project;
2. To authorize the County Manger to execute the Purchase Agreement and any necessary amendments;
3. To proceed with closing the transaction by waiving due diligence or accepting any encumbrances due to the emergency relief needed in order to close the transaction within thirty days; and
4. To authorize the Chairman to execute any necessary closing documents.
The estimated fiscal impact is $14,500 plus any due diligence costs that may still be required and closing costs (expenditure) and is within, and will not exceed, the Fiscal Year 2026 Budget. Commission District 4.

Recommend approval:
1. Of a Local Agency Program (LAP) agreement with the Florida Department of Transportation (FDOT) for funding the construction and construction inspection services for Britt Road, in the Mount Dora area;
2. To adopt a supporting Resolution for the LAP agreement;
3. To adopt an Unanticipated Revenue Resolution (URR) adding approximately $1,584,526 to the Federal/State Grants Fund for the construction of the Britt Road project; and
4. To authorize the Chairman to execute the documents when available to avoid further delay in project construction.
The estimated fiscal impact is $2,372,107 ($1,584,526 revenue/expenditure - grant funded; $514,581 ARPA funded expenditure; $273,000 Sales Tax expenditure). Commission District 4.

Recommend approval:
1. Of a Local Agency Program (LAP) agreement with the Florida Department of Transportation (FDOT);
2. To adopt a supporting Resolution with FDOT to fund the design services for the Hammock Ridge Road and Lakeshore Drive Roundabout;
3. To adopt an Unanticipated Revenue Resolution (URR) to accept and appropriate $375,000 to the Federal/State Grants Fund for the design services for the Hammock Ridge Road Roundabout Project; and
4. Of a budget transfer in the amount of $425,375 from South Transportation Benefit District Infrastructure-Construction to South Transportation Benefit District Infrastructure-Design Hammock Ridge Road Roundabout.
The estimated fiscal impact is $800,375–$375,000 (revenue/expenditure - grant funded), $425,375 (expenditure - county match). Commission District 1 and 2.

Recommend approval:
1. Of a Local Agency Program (LAP) agreement with the Florida Department of Transportation (FDOT);
2. To adopt a supporting Resolution with FDOT for funding the Right of Way acquisition of Log House Road (Pine Ridge Elementary School) from CR 561 to Lakeshore Drive;
3. To adopt an Unanticipated Revenue Resolution (URR) adding $150,000 to the Federal/State Grants Fund for the right of way acquisition services for the Log House Road Project; and
4. Of a budget transfer in the amount of $22,758 from Sidewalks to Easements/ROW Log House Rd (Pine Ridge Elem).
The estimated fiscal impact is $172,758 — $150,000 (revenue/expenditure — grant funded) and $22,758 (expenditure — county match). Commission District 1.

Recommend approval:
1. Of a Local Agency Program (LAP) agreement with the Florida Department of Transportation (FDOT);
2. To adopt a supporting Resolution with FDOT for Planning and Environmental Analysis Services (PD&E) for the Rolling Acres Road PD&E Study from south of CR 466 to north of US27/US441;
3. To adopt an Unanticipated Revenue Resolution (URR) adding $450,000 to the Federal/State Grants Fund for the Rolling Acres Road PD&E Project; and
4. Of a budget transfer in the amount of $450,000 from Easements/ROW to Infrastructure-Studies Rolling Acres Rd CR466 - US27/441.
The estimated fiscal impact is $900,000 — $450,000 (revenue/expenditure - grant funded) and $450,000 (expenditure - county match). Commission District 1 and 2.

Recommend approval:
1. To surplus County-owned property located at 31039 Lake Mack Road, in Deland (a/k/a Alternate Key 2540427), previously known as the Forest Hills Volunteer Fire Station;
2. To donate the property to Northeast Lake Community Outreach, Inc.;
3. Of the corresponding donation Resolution and authorization for the Chairman to execute the County Deed;
4. Of a Use Agreement to utilize the property as needed for emergency operations and authorization for the County Manager to execute the Agreement after the transfer of ownership is completed; and
5. To terminate the Lease Agreement upon transfer of the property.
This fiscal impact is $10 to record the County Deed. Commission District 4.

Recommend approval:
1. Of a County Incentive Grant Program (CIGP), agreement;
2. To adopt a supporting Resolution with the Florida Department of Transportation (FDOT) for funding the right-of-way acquisition services for Round Lake Road from the Lake/Orange County Line to Wolf Branch Road, in the Mount Dora area;
3. To adopt an Unanticipated Revenue Resolution (URR) adding $4,178,235 to the Federal/State Grants Fund for the right-of-way acquisition services for the Round Lake Road project; and
4. Of a budget transfer in the amount of $4,835,987 from Easements/ROW to Easements/ROW Round Lake Rd Seg 2.
The fiscal impact is $9,014,222 — $4,178,235 (revenue/expenditure - grant funded) and $4,835,987 (expenditure — county match). Commission District 4.

Recommend approval:1. Of a County Incentive Grant Program (CIGP) agreement;
2. To adopt a supporting Resolution with the Florida Department of Transportation (FDOT) for funding the construction services for the Hartwood Marsh Road Widening Project from US 27 to Majestic Isle Drive (aka Regency Hills Drive), in the Clermont area;
3. To adopt an Unanticipated Revenue Resolution (URR) to accept and appropriate $6,270,000 for the construction services for the Hartwood Marsh Road Widening Project; and
4. Of a budget transfer in the amount of $17,510,178 from South Transportation Benefit District Infrastructure-Construction to South Transportation Benefit District Infrastructure-Construction Hartwood Marsh Road.
The estimated fiscal impact is $29,373,478 — $6,270,000 (revenue/expenditure - grant funded) and $23,103,478 (expenditure - county match). Commission District 1 and 2.

Recommend approval:
1. To accept the Emergency Management Performance Grant (EMPG) Agreement;
2. To execute the EMPG Agreement for Fiscal Year 2025-2026 in the amount of $104,283;
3. To authorize the County Manager to execute future amendments/modifications that do not involve a financial impact;
4. To authorize the Director of the Office of Emergency Management to execute Exhibit 1 - Federal Programs and Resources Awarded (page 29);
5. To authorize the Director of the Office of Emergency Management to execute Exhibit 2 - Certification Regarding Telecommunications and Video Restrictions (pages 30 and 31);
6. To authorize the Director of the Office of Emergency Management to execute Exhibit 3 - Certification Regarding Lobbying (page 32); and
7. To adopt an Unanticipated Revenue Resolution (URR) to accept and appropriate $3,260 for the FY 2025-2026 EMPG Grant.
The fiscal impact is $104,283 (revenue/expenditure - grant funded).

Recommend approval:
1. To accept a Fiscal Year 2025-2026 Florida Department of Law Enforcement Drone Replacement Program Grant;
2. To authorize the Chairman to execute the Financial Assistance Agreement and Drone Funding Request Form required as part of the application;
3. To authorize the County Manager to approve the drone purchase after County Attorney review; and
4. To authorize the Office of Procurement Services to execute all supporting documentation.
The fiscal impact is $150,000 (revenue/expenditure – grant funded) and is within the Fiscal Year 2026 Budget.

Recommend approval:
1. To accept a Federal Emergency Management Agency's 2024 Staffing for Adequate Fire and Emergency Response (SAFER) grant to hire 36 firefighters;
2. To expend the required $4,664,578.18 County match;
3. To adopt an Unrecognized Revenue Resolution (URR) to accept and appropriate $3,042,117 in Year 1 grant funds; and
4. To purchase 36 sets of protective gear and uniforms at an estimated cost of $286,200.
The total estimated fiscal impact over three years is $12,454,664.76 - $7,503,886.58 (revenue/expenditure - grant funded), $4,664,578.18 (expenditure - county match) and $286,200 (expenditure) for a one time purchase of gear and uniforms.

Recommend approval:
1. Of the Third Amendment to the Lake County Water Authority (LCWA) Management Services Agreement;
2. To proceed with the transfer of the Hickory Point Recreation Complex (Alternate Key 1208190) from the LCWA to the County, including conducting due diligence and to closing upon receipt of clear title;
3. Of a Resolution authorizing the transfer of the Pasture Reserve (Alternate Keys 1029848, 1103011, and 3836181), the Lady Lake Preserve (Alternate Key 1739683), and the Helena Run Preserve (Alternate Keys 1383391 and 1294134) to LCWA; and authorization to execute the corresponding County Deed;
4. To transfer the management of the Pine Meadows Conservation Area (Alternate Key 1017416) and the Blueway Trail System to LCWA; and
5. To authorize the Chairman to execute any necessary transfer or closing documents to facility these transactions.
There is no fiscal impact.

Recommend approval:1. To accept the Emergency Management Preparedness and Assistance (EMPA) Trust Fund Base Grant;
2. To execute the EMPA Trust Fund Base Grant Agreement for Fiscal Year 2025-2026 in the amount of $105,806;
3. To authorize the County Manager to execute future amendments/modifications that do not involve a financial impact;
4. To authorize the Director of the Office of Emergency Management to execute Exhibit 1 - Single Audits, Audit Compliance Certification (page 21); and
5. To authorize the Director of the Office of Emergency Management to execute Exhibit 2 - 27-19 Florida Administrative Code, EM Director or Part-Time Coordinator Certification (page 22).
The fiscal impact is $105,806 (revenue/expenditure - grant funded) and will be included in the Fiscal Year 2026 Budget.

Recommend approval:1. Of a Substantial Amendment of the Community Development Block Grant (CDBG) Fiscal Year 2024-2025 Annual Action Plan;
2. To authorize the Office of Housing & Community Services Director to execute any documents related to the Substantial Amendment required by the U.S. Department of Housing & Urban Development;
3. Of a corresponding authorizing Resolution that adopts the Substantial Amendment to the CDBG Fiscal Year 2024-2025 Annual Action Plan;
4. Of a budget transfer in the amount of $54,000 reallocating funding from the Stuckey Community Center Project to the New Beginnings Roof Replacement Project; and
5. Of the Subrecipient Agreement with New Beginnings to replace the roofs on two existing homes, one single-family and one duplex, being used as transitional housing for low-income families in need.
The fiscal impact is $54,000 (expenditure). Commission District 2.

Recommend approval:1. Of a Substantial Amendment of the Community Development Block Grant (CDBG) Fiscal Year 2019-2020 Annual Action Plan;
2. To authorize the Office of Housing & Community Services Director to execute any documents required by the U.S. Department of Housing & Urban Development;
3. Of a corresponding authorizing Resolution that adopts the Substantial Amendment to the CDBG Fiscal Year 2019-2020 Annual Action Plan; and
4. Of a budget transfer in the amount of $222,561 reallocating CDBG-CV3 Capital funds from CV-Rent and Mortgage Assistance to Food Assistance.
The fiscal impact is $222,561 (expenditure - grant funded).

Recommend approval to authorize the County Manager to execute payments for economic development incentive grants due under the applicable agreements to: The Kroger Company of Ohio for the Economic Development Grant Incentive Program in the amount of $89,134.42;
The Kroger Company of Ohio for the Agreement for Waiver of Impact Fees in the amount of $49,105.00;
Ocado Solutions USA Inc. for the Economic Development Grant Incentive Program in the amount of $195,706.32;
Encompass Health for the Economic Development Grant Incentive Program in the amount of $73,485.52;and
Encompass Health for the Agreement for Waiver of Impact Fees in the amount of $45,089.32.
The fiscal impact is $452,520.58 (expenditure) and is within, and will not exceed, the Fiscal Year 2025 Budget.

Recommend approval:1. Of a Local Agency Program (LAP) agreement with the Florida Department of Transportation (FDOT);
2. To adopt a supporting resolution with the FDOT for construction services for the Villa City Road safety project in the Groveland and Mascotte area;
3. Of a budget transfer in the amount of $3,648,865 from South Transportation Benefit District Infrastructure Construction to South Transportation Benefit District Infrastructure Construction for the Villa City Road project; and
4. Of an Unanticipated Revenue Resolution (URR) to accept and appropriate $3,305,187 for the Villa City Road Safety Project.
The estimated fiscal impact is $6,954,052 - $3,305,187 (revenue/expenditure - grant funded) and $3,648,865 (expenditure - county responsibility). Commission District 1.

Recommend approval:1. To accept and authorize the administration of a U.S. Department of Energy ("DOE") Energy Efficiency and Conservation Block Grant ("EECBG") agreement;
2. Of Contract 24-952 with D.E. Scorpio Corporation (Gainesville, FL) for the Federally Funded Window Replacement Project;
3. Of an Unanticipated Revenue Resolution (URR) to accept and appropriate $322,790 for the DOE EECBG Program Award; and
4. To authorize the Office of Procurement Services to execute all supporting documentation.
The estimated fiscal impact is $510,007 - $322,790 (revenue/expenditure - grant funded) and $187,217 (expenditure - county funded). Commission District 3.

Recommend approval:
1. Of a Memorandum of Agreement (MOA) for Lake County's continued participation in the Orlando Urban Areas Security Initiative (UASI);
2. To authorize the Chairman to execute the MOA;
3. To designate the Office of Emergency Management Director as the primary representative for Lake County to the UASI Working Group; and
4. To authorize the County Manager to execute any future no-cost amendments or modifications to the MOA.
There is no fiscal impact.
